From 7134f3f02fd545fcdfb1baacd687763bfcd5b531 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Michael C. Ferguson" <michael.christopher.ferguson@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 20:00:29 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] usb: dwc2: host: keep a full-speed interrupt channel
 running across URBs

In descriptor DMA mode an interrupt QH gets a channel when a URB is
queued, its list ends in EOL, and the channel halts and is released on
every completion. The next URB is armed on a new channel. On a DWC_otg
2.93a with the root port capped to full speed (fs_ddma), a 1 kHz mouse
(bInterval 1, one URB in flight) is polled every other frame, 500
reports/s, and four URBs in flight only reach 667/s. The descriptor
retires about 74 us into frame N, the halt and giveback take about
40 us and the resubmit about 60 us more, and the re-armed channel is
only served in frame N+2: the core has committed frame N+1 by the time
the re-arm lands.

With dma_desc_int_chain, keep the channel of a full- or low-speed
interrupt QH running instead:

- Arm the list without EOL, one descriptor per URB, each with IOC, and
  NTD covering all 64 slots. Unmask XferCompl and BNA for the channel.
- On enqueue, write the URB into the next free slot of the running
  list: buf, dma_wmb(), then the status word with A set. Only slots at
  or after qh->td_last are written, never a descriptor the core owns,
  and only while the channel is started with no halt pending and
  HCCHAR.CHENA still reads set, under the lock.
- On XferCompl without a halt, give back the QTDs whose descriptors
  were retired without error, in order, and leave the channel running.
- When the list runs dry the core fetches a descriptor without A set,
  latches BNA and disables the channel, with no CHHLTD. BNA with CHENA
  clear is taken as the channel having stopped: the retired QTDs are
  given back, the toggle is saved from HCTSIZ, and the channel is
  re-armed from slot 0 with the QTDs still queued, or released if none
  is. A descriptor still marked active was not serviced, so an append
  that lost the race with the fetch is re-armed as well. An append that
  finds the channel already stopped writes nothing and leaves its QTD
  to the pending BNA or CHHLTD interrupt, which re-arms from qtd_list.
- The last slot carries EOL and closes the list, as does a URB that
  needs more than one descriptor, which is armed the old way.

The halt handling changes to go with this, for chained channels only. A
CHHLTD with XferCompl is scanned once, from the halt path, and as an
error halt if an error cause is latched too: every slot has IOC, so the
XferCompl may be an earlier slot's. A halt with no error cause completes
the list instead of being taken for an incomplete periodic transfer, and
re-arms the channel from slot 0 if URBs are queued, which also restarts
the ring after its EOL. A halt that arrives after the last QTD was given
back releases the channel instead of only masking the interrupt, which
would leak it. chan->qh is set before dwc2_hc_init() so that the
interrupt setup can see the QH.

dwc2_hc_halt() does not touch a channel that is already disabled, so
a dequeue halt of a channel stopped by BNA would wait for a CHHLTD
that never comes, the channel stays assigned and the endpoint never
runs again. When no CHHLTD is latched, a dequeue or an endpoint reset
completes the halt at once. While a dequeue halt is pending BNA stays
unmasked, since the channel may stop on BNA before it acts on CHDIS;
the channel is then only released once a trailing CHHLTD has had a
few microseconds to land, so that it cannot hit the next owner.

A chained channel with no URB queued stays enabled until the core
fetches the next slot, so three places stop it: endpoint disable,
through dwc2_hcd_qh_free_ddma(), which halts the QH's channel before
freeing the list; an endpoint reset, which halts the channel so that
it restarts from DATA0, since the running channel holds the PID and
the halt would otherwise save it over the reset toggle; and a bus or
port suspend that powers the core down and loses the channel state,
which halts and releases such channels first.

On the 2.93a, with a full-speed hub, a gamepad and a mouse behind it:
- a descriptor fetched without A set stops the channel with BNA and
  CHENA clear but no CHHLTD. Waiting for the halt, as the first version
  of this change did, left every chained endpoint stopped after one
  report with its URB outstanding;
- the hub's status endpoint, whose 1-byte reports fill the descriptor,
  got XferCompl without a halt for a mid-list IOC descriptor and kept
  its channel enabled across URBs. The gamepad and mouse IN endpoints,
  with short reports, and the gamepad's OUT endpoint had BNA latched
  together with XferCompl: the core fetched the next slot right after
  retiring one, so with one URB in flight these stop on every URB and
  run as before;
- after one packet from DATA0, HCTSIZ.PID read DATA1 once the channel
  had stopped on BNA.

What this can gain therefore depends on when the core reads the next
descriptor. With two or more URBs in flight, the slot after the one
being served is normally already active, so the endpoint should be
polled every frame, apart from one restart per 64 URBs at the end of
the ring. With one URB in flight, as usbhid runs, the channel is only
kept when the core fetches the next slot late enough for the resubmit
to land first.

Still not verified on hardware:
- no write-back of the status word of a descriptor fetched with A
  clear. If the core does write it back, an append that loses the race
  can have its A bit cleared, and the scan would complete the IN URB
  with stale NBYTES;
- at most one descriptor served per scheduled frame on a full-speed
  interrupt channel in scatter-gather mode. A core that walks all
  active descriptors in one frame, as for bulk, would issue several IN
  tokens per frame with more than one URB in flight;
- which of BNA and a CHHLTD the core raises when the list runs dry
  while a CHDIS is pending.

Signed-off-by: Michael C. Ferguson <michael.christopher.ferguson@gmail.com>
